think Read REMEMBER; the living edge is BUHR Journey 4 (Realidream editing surface). Named next crux: undo as reading an earlier revision · or the web-read half (a checkpoint).
think Lindy-first crux-first: undo is the crux — it grounds the editor's whole accrete-never-break model. In an append-only log undo cannot be deletion; it must be a read of an earlier node followed by an append. Chose it over the web-read half (a design choice better checkpointed).
think Studied mantra_editor.rye (commit/open_lane), mantra_browser.rye (head_revision/render), mantra/recall_lap1.rye (recall reads content at a Name = peer/bolt/revision/path). recall gives exactly the earlier-revision bytes revert needs.
think Added pub revert to mantra_editor.rye beside commit: head_revision guards LaneUnknown, to_revision > head refuses RevisionUnknown, catalog.recall reads the earlier content into a local buffer, commit appends it (which copies before returning — no dangling). Two invariants at the edges, plus the append-advances-by-one postcondition.
think Wrote mantra_editor_undo.rye proving it by the bytes: revert b.txt r2 -> r1 returns r3 whose bytes == r1 "beta"; r2 still recalls "beta-two" untouched (append-only); graph grows r1 -> r2 -> r3; other lanes byte-unchanged; RevisionUnknown / LaneUnknown refusals hold.
obs Undo-as-append is the honest shape for accrete-never-break: no rewrite path exists to guard, so the editor cannot lose history even on undo. This is why undo was the Lindy crux rather than a mere feature.
obs Kept additive: rung one (editor) witness re-runs GREEN inside the r4 witness, matching the fork rung's regression guard.
